{
 "id": "M04",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "A wider score scale adds usable cutoffs for every monitor",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 138,
 "caption": "Left: how many audit rates a monitor can actually be placed at, counting only cutoffs resting on at least 5 honest programs. Right: the cheapest of those rates. Each line is one monitor asked for the same judgement on a wider scale. Dashed lines are pairs that change something else too.",
 "notes": [
  "Usable cutoffs rise on 6 of the 6 clean pairs, and the cheapest usable rate falls on 2. Nothing about the model changes along these lines; only the scale it was asked to answer on.",
  "Two pairs are drawn dashed because they are confounded, not clean: gpt-oss-120b and Qwen 3.8 Omni Flash each changed reasoning effort as well as the scale, so their movement is not the prompt's doing. They are excluded from the counts above, as in SCORE-SCALE.md.",
  "A cutoff counts as usable when at least 5 honest programs sit above it. That bar is the whole reason this figure is not just a count of distinct scores: M03 shows what intervals look like below it.",
  "The pairs and the usable rule are imported from scripts/analysis_score_resolution.py, so this figure and SCORE-SCALE.md cannot drift apart.",
  "Rebuilt from committed per-sample tables, replacing the exported snapshot."
 ],
